WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.277
Hi, my name's Sid.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.480
I run an exterior-cleaning
business in the UK.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.315
And today, I'm going to
be taking you through

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:17.283
cleaning a very mossy roof.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.686
So, this roof hasn't been
cleaned for the 28 years

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:21.087
that the homeowners
have lived here.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:27.927
Here, I'm beginning
to scrape the roof.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:30.897
So, I'm trying to remove 
as much moss as possible.

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.866
This is what I'll be doing
for the bulk of the day,

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:35.168
and this is the hardest
part of the job.

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:36.336
I have the tool that I use.

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:38.738
It's basically a
flat metal blade

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:39.906
angled to one side,

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:42.075
which means I can get
the edge of the tile,

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:44.177
which usually carries
a lot of the moss,

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:46.279
and scrape the bulk surface

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:47.614
of the tile at
the same time.

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:49.182
I use sort of a 
side-to-side motion,

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:50.717
and it is very 
taxing on the body.

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:52.352
So it takes a long
time to build up

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:55.155
the endurance and 
strength to do it.

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:56.022
So, on this roof, you'll see

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:57.390
a lot of brown and green.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.224
This is the moss.

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:59.325
You'll also see a 
lot of white spots

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:01.060
people think is
bird droppings,

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.463
but this is white lichen.

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:06.132
Moss is terrible for 
roofing and roof materials,

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:07.500
especially in the UK,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.436
as we get a lot of damp
weather over here.

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:11.037
So it just tends to 
grow and fester more,

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:13.406
and it'll grow in between
the cracks of the tiles.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.474
Now, when you 
have hot weather

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:15.508
turn into cold weather,

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.210
the moss will 
freeze and expand

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.611
and then end up cracking
the roof tiles,

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.480
which can create
holes, leaks,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.182
and things like 
that in the roof.

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:23.516
So, this part of the job,

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.618
where I'm on the scaffolding,
was the hardest.

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:27.987
So I use a 40-feet
extendable pole

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:29.689
with the scraper 
attached on the end,

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.825
and I was almost 
at max extension.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:33.660
So, the scraper itself
is quite heavy,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.562
and the pole itself
is quite heavy.

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:37.797
So having that out at a
near horizontal level

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:40.033
for a couple of hours
is very difficult.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.134
And as soon as
this was done,

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:42.435
I was relieved and happy

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.304
to move on to the next part.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:45.538
So, what I'm cleaning here

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.941
is a four-bedroomed house.

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:50.210
It's got big, flat
concrete tiles.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.711
So these are some
of the best tiles

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:52.912
to come across to clean.

00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:53.913
The surface area of them,

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:55.448
and because they're
completely flat,

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.016
it's very easy to
get the moss off.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.052
You do not get roofs
better than this.

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:00.954
So, it's rare that you'll
actually get away with

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.089
using a broom to
clean a roof,

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:05.358
as usually the moss is
very, very stuck on,

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:07.327
and it's typically just
used for cleaning up.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:08.261
However, on this job,

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:09.395
the moss was 
coming off easy,

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:10.497
so in certain sections,

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:11.831
it can just be easier

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:13.566
just to try and 
sweep off a majority

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:16.002
and then sort of fine-tune
it with a scraper.

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:22.775
So, the typical 
cleaning chemicals

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:24.244
that I use in my 
roof-cleaning work

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:26.646
is a chemical 
called a biocide.

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:29.082
What this essentially
will do is destroy

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:31.184
any of the organic 
growth left on the roof,

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:32.886
and it has a residual
cleaning effect

00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:33.853
once I'm gone as well.

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:34.921
So there's no 
rinsing it off.

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.422
You leave it on the
roof to fully dry

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:37.924
and soak into the tiles.

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:38.791
This will get anything

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.226
that we can't get
to by scraping,

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.794
so anything that's
left in the gaps.

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:44.063
Any sort of really 
stubborn stains and marks

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.699
that are left on the tiles
also will slowly degrade.

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:48.034
So, the brush that
I'm using here

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:49.602
is a basic water-fed pole

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:51.204
that most window 
cleaners would use.

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:52.038
That's all you need.

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:53.406
It's got four jets in it,

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:55.341
which will fire out
the chemical mix

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.543
with the water
onto the roof.

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:57.577
And the brush is just used

00:02:57.577 --> 00:02:58.778
to agitate that chemical,

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:00.647
which will help it foam
up, as you can see.

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:02.448
This helps it stick
to the roof more,

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.350
as it's a natural
surfactant.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.485
So, what I'm doing here

00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:08.221
is I'm applying the biocide
solution to the roof.

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:11.658
Now, this is diluted
at 25:1 with water.

00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:13.226
So for every 1 
liter of biocide,

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:16.329
I use 25 liters of 
water to dilute it,

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:17.330
ensuring I'm covering

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.831
every square inch
of this roof,

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.633
making sure it's 
completely soaked in.

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:22.035
A lot of mistakes
people make

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:23.269
is not putting enough on,

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:25.538
and they're left with
a very patchy roof.

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:28.007
So it's really important
to get complete coverage

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:29.742
and make sure all of
the tiny little gaps

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:31.611
are completely soaked.

00:03:31.611 --> 00:03:33.580
So if you don't fill
all of the gaps

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:34.981
on the roof with
the chemical,

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:37.116
then the moss will 
regrow with a vengeance.

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:38.518
The important part
is making sure

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:39.786
anything organic
on this roof

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:42.322
is completely absorbing
this chemical.

00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:44.958
So once the biocide completely
soaks and dries in,

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:46.492
you would never even 
know it was on there.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.928
And your results will start
to come after a few weeks.

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:50.763
The main thing is 
trusting in the chemical,

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:52.498
because it definitely
will do its job.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:58.972
So, it's important to 
have your roof cleaned

00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:00.006
because the buildup of moss

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:02.909
will over time damage 
the tiles on the roof,

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:05.745
which can lead to 
cracks, damp, leak.

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.113
All sorts of issues
in your home

00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:09.015
that you really don't
want happening.

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:11.951
And if the problem is not
treated early enough,

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.419
then it can lead 
to very expensive

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.588
roof repairs in the future.

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.190
As far as time scale on

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.592
how often should my
roof be cleaned,

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:21.728
it is pretty much
a one-time deal

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:23.363
for the majority
of the process,

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:25.164
as long as it's being
kept on top of.

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:26.266
And in future, 
it would just be

00:04:26.266 --> 00:04:27.367
a light chemical treatment

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:28.801
just to keep 
anything at bay.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.603
So the first step 
is getting it done,

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.038
and then it's a case
of maintaining it.

